// Enaga: <cmath> for apps: std::sin and the rest on float, from <math.h> (include/libm/math.h, runtime/libm.c),
// within 1 ulp. Only float: the double overloads are deleted, so std::sin(1.0) is a compile error rather than a
// quietly slow double computation; write std::sin(1.0f), or float variables. An integer argument (std::sqrt(2)) is
// ambiguous for the same reason. For inner loops, enaga::dsp's math (<enaga/dsp/math.hpp>) is several times faster.
